**Design options for `ErrForma`:**

Option 1 (flat enum, new members):
```julia
@enum Err begin
    ...
    ErrFormaEvidenciaCbidAjeno
    ErrFormaOrdenCanonicoInvalido
    ...
end
```
Then RES strings are `ErrFormaEvidenciaCbidAjeno`. Doesn't match `ErrForma(EvidenciaCbidAjeno)`.

Option 2 (flat enum with two-level via a struct and an abstract error supertype):
```julia
abstract type ErrorTransicion end

@enum Err <...>  # but @enum creates concrete type; can't subtype
```
In Julia, `@enum Err begin ... end` defines `Err` as a concrete primitive type. You cannot make it a subtype of an abstract type. So Option 2 requires redefining `Err` not as an enum but as... hmm. We could keep `Err` enum for semantic errors and introduce a separate `ErrForma` struct, plus a union alias:
```julia
@enum Err begin ... end  # remove ErrCbidAjeno, ErrOrdenCanonico
@enum ClaseForma EvidenciaCbidAjeno OrdenCanonicoInvalido
struct ErrForma
    clase::ClaseForma
end
const ErrorTransicion = Union{Err, ErrForma}
```
Then all checks `r isa Err` must become `r isa ErrorTransicion` (or `!(r === nothing)`). This is the faithful representation. But it changes the public type `Err`? No, `Err` remains the enum; `ErrForma` is new. The return type is now a union. T04 has `Dict{...,Transicion.Err}` annotations: it would fail when trying to store an `ErrForma` (TypeError: cannot convert ErrForma to Err). So T04 breaks at runtime for form-error cases — which O2 must fix. If T04 is running concurrently, O2 needs to know. But O2's order is the same as mine and explicitly requires T04 to treat these as block-invalid, so O2 will necessarily touch those annotations. The concern is only about *timing*: if O2 is reading T01's `Transicion.jl` mid-flight, it might see a partial state. Since I edit files atomically with `write`/`edit`, and T04 `include`s the file at process start, a concurrent T04 Julia process that already started has already loaded the old code; if it starts after my edit, it loads the new code. Either way it gets a consistent file (edits are not partial in a way Julia would read mid-write? `write` truncates then writes; a reader could observe an empty/partial file if it reads exactly during the write. Risk is low but real.)

